%S A400512 0,1,8,57,373,2342,14343,86497,516488,3064077,18099433,106603982,
%T A400512 626662803,3678916837,21578229848,126487060017,741131755693,
%U A400512 4341315304022,25425120200463,148883717129977,871750345082408,5103994944782757,29882024984209153,174943293784492862
%N A400512 Expansion of g.f. x*(1-x)/((1-4*x)*(1-5*x-5*x^2)).
%C A400512 In the linked Equilateral Completion paper, a(n) is the number of product-centered local equilateral triangles in order-n floretion triangular coordinates.
%H A400512 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400512/b400512.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%H A400512 Creighton Dement, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15925">Equilateral Completion in Floretion Triangular Coordinates: Locality, Product Points, and Reflection Symmetry</a>, arXiv:2608.15925 [math.CO], 2026. See Theorem 6.2 and Appendix C.
%H A400512 Creighton Dement, <a href="https://www.floretions.com">Online Floretion Calculator</a>; see also the OEIS tab.
%H A400512 Creighton Dement, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.20265">Bitwise Triangular Coordinates for Central Products of Quaternion Groups: Floretion Base Vectors, Digitwise S3-Actions, and Centralizer Tiles</a>, arXiv:2605.20265 [math.GM], 2026.
%H A400512 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (9,-15,-20).
%F A400512 a(n) = (H(n)-4^n)/3, where H(0)=1, H(1)=7, and H(n) = 5*H(n-1)+5*H(n-2) = A057088(n)+2*A057088(n-1) for n >= 2.
%F A400512 a(n) = 9*a(n-1)-15*a(n-2)-20*a(n-3), n >= 3, with a(0)=0, a(1)=1, a(2)=8.
%F A400512 E.g.f.: exp(5*x/2)*(5*cosh(3*sqrt(5)*x/2) + 3*sqrt(5)*sinh(3*sqrt(5)*x/2) - 5*exp(3*x/2))/15. - _Stefano Spezia_, Sep 28 2026
%e A400512 For n=3, the transfer recurrence gives H(3)=235 admissible pairs when empty selections are included. Removing the 4^3=64 empty selections leaves 171 pairs. These form 171/3=57 orbits, so a(3)=57. For example, T = {eii,ejj,ekk} = T(eii,{2,3}) is one of these triangles. Its product point and Euclidean center both equal P(eee).
%t A400512 LinearRecurrence[{9, -15, -20}, {0, 1, 8}, 25] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 05 2026 *)
